.TH PKERNEL 8 2026-10-25 DarqOS "DarqOS System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
pkernel \- DarqOS pseudo-kernel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pkernel
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pkernel
is the Linux service that provides the functionality that will
eventually live in the DarqOS kernel.
It keeps the process table, manages IPC ports and each process'
handles for them, keeps the name registry through which processes find
each other's ports, and routes IPC messages between DarqOS processes.
.PP
Every DarqOS process holds a TCP connection to
.B pkernel
on the loopback interface, and uses it to make pseudo-system calls
(p-calls) such as
.BR open_port ,
.BR send_message ,
.B spawn_process
and
.BR kill_process .
.B pkernel
is not itself a DarqOS process: it has no DarqOS process identifier
(dpid).
.PP
On startup,
.B pkernel
listens on TCP port 11000 on the loopback interface (127.0.0.1),
schedules the boot sequence, and runs its event loop until shut down.
.PP
The boot sequence spawns, in order, the Storage service
.RI ( services/storage ),
the Type service
.RI ( services/type ),
and the type sync
.RI ( tools/type-sync ),
which registers the installed types and lenses with the Type service
and then exits.
They're found under the directory above the p-kernel's own,
.IR /darq .
They're spawned at once, without waiting for each other: each
process' blocking name lookup waits for the service it needs.
One that can't be started is logged, and the rest are started anyway.
.SS Processes
A process is created by a
.B spawn_process
p-call.
.B pkernel
allocates a dpid, then starts
.BR pyloader (8)
from its own directory with that dpid, the parent's dpid, the
listening port, and the executable path.
The executable path must be a directory containing a
.I main.py
file.
The child runs with
.I /
as its working directory, and with the directory containing the
.B darq
package that
.B pkernel
is using prepended to
.BR PYTHONPATH .
.PP
The new process connects back and asks to run with a RUN_REQUEST
message.
.B pkernel
accepts it only for a dpid it has spawned and that has not yet
started: it answers with a RUN_RESPONSE, and only then is the
.B spawn_process
call answered.
.PP
A
.B spawn_process
call can name a
.I bootstrap
handle, for which the caller must hold a right: if it doesn't, or the
port is dead, the call fails at once with BAD_BOOTSTRAP, and nothing is
started.
When the new process runs, it's given a send right to that port, and
its handle in the RUN_RESPONSE; the handle is 0 if none was named, or
the port has been destroyed since.
For an unknown dpid, or one that is not newly spawned, the
RUN_RESPONSE refuses the request and the connection is closed.
Any other first message also causes the connection to be closed.
.PP
When a process' application finishes, it reports its exit status, and
a description of any failure, in an EXITED message, then closes its
connection.
.B pkernel
records both in the process table.
.PP
A finished process (ZOMBIE or LOST) stays in the process table until
its parent collects it with the
.B wait
p-call, which reports whether it exited (with its status and error),
was killed, or was lost.
A process the p-kernel spawned itself stays for the p-kernel.
A finished process whose parent has also finished is an orphan, which
nothing can collect: it's removed at once.
An orphan keeps its parent's dpid (dpids are never reused), rather than
becoming the p-kernel's.
.PP
Exited host processes are reaped on
.BR SIGCHLD ,
including those of processes already removed from the table.
If a child exits before it sends RUN_REQUEST, its
.B spawn_process
call is answered with an error, and it's removed.
.SS Dead port notifications
A
.B notify_on_death
p-call names a handle to watch, and a notify port, which must be one of
the caller's receive rights.
When the watched port is destroyed (its receive right is closed, or its
owner finishes),
.B pkernel
delivers a PortDestroyed notification to the notify port, from source
handle 0, carrying the caller's handle for the dead port.
A port that is already dead is notified at once, before the response.
Closing the watched handle, or the notify port, cancels the
notification.
.SS Timeouts
A spawned process that hasn't sent RUN_REQUEST within 10 seconds has
its host process killed (and an error logged), so its
.B spawn_process
call fails.
A killed process that hasn't disconnected within 5 seconds has its
host process killed (and an error logged), so its
.B kill_process
call succeeds.
A process killed before it sends RUN_REQUEST has its host process
killed at once.
Blocking name lookups don't time out, but one still waiting is logged
as a warning every 30 seconds.
.SS Shutdown and reboot
A
.B shutdown
or
.B reboot
p-call from a process the p-kernel spawned itself (parent dpid 0), or
an interrupt (SIGINT), shuts the system down; the p-call from any other
process is ignored, and logged.
Shutting down, every running
process is killed, as by
.BR kill_process ,
and spawned processes not yet running have their host processes
killed.
Processes still running after 5 seconds have their host processes
killed.
Once none are left,
.B pkernel
exits with status 0 for a shutdown, or replaces itself with a new
p-kernel (with
.BR execve (2))
for a reboot.
Replacing itself, rather than starting another process and exiting,
lets
.BR systemd (1)
see the same service continue.
Spawning is refused during shutdown.
.SS Boot vector
The p-kernel keeps a small, persistent name:value store, the
.IR "boot vector" ,
for the processes it spawned itself (parent dpid 0): with no boot
volume, it's where system services keep what they need to find their
own state, such as the keys of their storage blobs.
It holds at most 64 names, of up to 255 bytes, with values of up to
1024 bytes.
Each change is saved, by writing a temporary file and renaming it,
before the p-call is answered.
A boot vector file that can't be read is renamed with a
.I .bad
suffix, and the p-kernel starts with an empty one.
.SS Process states
.TP
.B ALLOCATED
Table entry created, but the process is not yet spawned.
.TP
.B SPAWNED
The loader has been started, but has not sent RUN_REQUEST.
.TP
.B RUNNING
The process is connected and able to make p-calls.
.TP
.B ZOMBIE
The process disconnected after reporting its exit, or after being
killed; its parent has not yet collected it.
.TP
.B LOST
The process disconnected without reporting its exit or being killed;
its parent has not yet collected it.
.SH OPTIONS
None.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B INVOCATION_ID
If set (as it is by
.BR systemd (1)),
log messages are written to standard output without timestamps, for
the journal.
Otherwise they are written to standard error with timestamps.
.TP
.B PYTHONPATH
Passed on to spawned processes, after the
.B darq
package directory.
.TP
.B DARQ_RUN
The run directory, for files changed at runtime, such as services'
databases and the boot vector; by default,
.IR /darq/run .
A relative path, such as
.I ./run
in a development tree, is taken from the p-kernel's working directory.
It's created if needed, and spawned processes get its absolute path in
.BR DARQ_RUN .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /darq/kernel/pkernel
The p-kernel executable.
.TP
.I /darq/kernel/pyloader
Loader for Python executables, started for each new process.
.TP
.I /darq/run/bootvec.json
The boot vector, as JSON, with values in hex.
.TP
.I /darq/kernel/darqos-pkernel.service
.BR systemd (1)
unit that runs the p-kernel as part of
.IR darqos.target .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 after a shutdown.
.SH BUGS
The listening port is fixed at 11000.
.PP
The boot sequence doesn't yet start the Index, History or Terminal
services.
.PP
Finished processes the p-kernel spawned itself are never removed from
the process table.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pyloader (8),
.BR install (8),
.BR systemd (1)
